    text: `Ender had a dream that he was a giant standing over a shattered world, holding a stethoscope in his hands. In the dream, he searched for something alive among the ruins, something that could tell the story of what had been destroyed, something that could give voice to the silence that had replaced the voices of the dead. The dream haunted him because he could not understand its meaning, could not connect it to anything he knew about his life or his training or his responsibilities as a commander in Battle School. It was as if his subconscious was trying to tell him something important, something that his conscious mind was not yet ready to hear or understand. The dream repeated itself over and over, each time with slight variations, each time leaving him with a sense of unease that he could not shake, a feeling that he was being prepared for something he did not yet understand. Years later, when he stood over the dead Formic world, he finally understood. The dream was a vision of his future role as the Speaker for the Dead, the one who would listen to the last thoughts of a dying species and tell their story to the universe, the one who would give voice to those who could no longer speak for themselves and ensure that their lives and their deaths were not forgotten. The giant was not a destroyer but a seeker, not an ending but a beginning, a symbol of the transformation that Ender would undergo as he moved from being a soldier to being a storyteller, from being a weapon to being a witness, from being someone who ended lives to being someone who preserved the memory of those lives. The stethoscope was a tool of listening, of hearing the faint sounds of life that remained even in the most devastated circumstances, of finding the pulse of existence in places where it seemed impossible that anything could still be alive. Ender would carry this tool metaphorically throughout his life, using it to listen to the stories of dying species, to hear the echoes of civilizations that had been destroyed, to find the threads of connection that linked the past to the future and gave meaning to the suffering of the present. The shattered world in the dream was not just the Formic home world but every world that had been destroyed by war, every civilization that had been lost to violence and ignorance and fear, and Ender's mission was to ensure that none of them were forgotten, that their stories were told and retold and carried forward into the future as warnings and as inspirations for those who would come after. The dream was a gift from his deeper self, a preview of the purpose that would guide his life for centuries, a promise that his suffering and his guilt and his isolation would not be in vain, that they would be transformed into something meaningful and enduring that would benefit generations of beings who would never know the pain that had created the wisdom they would inherit. Ender did not fully understand the dream until much later, but even in the moments of his greatest confusion and despair, he trusted it, trusted that it was leading him somewhere important, trusting that the giant in his dream was showing him the path he needed to follow even when that path was difficult and uncertain and filled with obstacles that seemed insurmountable.`
